- How would you adapt for 128-bit inputs and performance considerations?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to extend classic bit-trick patterns to wider word sizes without branches, a common need in performance-critical systems.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register)
- 128-bit data layout with two 64-bit halves
- Branchless bit counting and aggregation

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

static inline uint64_t popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

int popcount128(uint128 a) {
  return (int)(popcount64(a.hi) + popcount64(a.lo));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for SIMD-enabled languages (e.g., intrinsics)?
- Compare branchless approach vs. lookup-based methods on large datasets.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to manipulate a 128-bit value by composing 64-bit operations, enforcing a non-loop, fixed-sequence approach for performance.

## Key Concepts
- Endianness and byte-level reversal
- Bit-twiddling for 64-bit reversal
- Combining two 64-bit halves into a 128-bit result

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t v) {
  v = ((v & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8) | ((v >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
  v = ((v & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((v >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
  v = (v << 32) | (v >> 32);
  return v;
}
uint128 swapEndian128(uint128 x) {
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = reverse64(x.hi);
  r.hi = reverse64(x.lo);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if only a single 128-bit type isn’t available?
- How would you validate correctness with representative test patterns (all zeros, all ones, alternating bits)?

## Why This Is Asked

- Checks 128-bit handling and branchless bit counting, a common crypto/DB pattern.
- Evaluates ability to compose primitives without loops.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) popcount on 64-bit chunks
- Splitting 128-bit into hi/lo and aggregating results
- Constant-time, no branches

## Code Example

```javascript
unsigned int popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
