## Why This Is Asked
This tests advanced bit manipulation skills, requiring multi-word arithmetic without 128-bit native support. It demonstrates branchless programming techniques and careful handling of shift operations.

## Key Concepts
- Represent 128-bit values as two 64-bit words (hi, lo)
- Decompose k into t = k mod 64 (intra-word shift) and b = k div 64 (cross-word flag)
- Branchless selection using bitwise operations and masks
- Handle edge cases like k=0 and k=64 without conditionals

## Code Example
```c
void rotate_left_128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, unsigned k, 
                    uint64_t* out_hi, uint64_t* out_lo) {
    uint64_t t = k & 63;
    uint64_t b = (k >> 6) & 1;

    uint64_t hiA = (hi << t) | (lo >> (64 - t));
    uint64_t loA = (lo << t) | (hi >> (64 - t));
    uint64_t hiB = (lo << t) | (hi >> (64 - t));
    uint64_t loB = (hi << t) | (lo >> (64 - t));

    *out_hi = hiA ^ ((hiA ^ hiB) & -b);
    *out_lo = loA ^ ((loA ^ loB) & -b);
}
```

## Examples
- k=1, hi=0, lo=1 → out_hi=1, out_lo=0
- k=64, hi=1, lo=0 → out_hi=0, out_lo=1

## Why This Is Asked
This evaluates your ability to extend bit manipulation techniques to multi-register data structures while maintaining branchless performance.

## Key Concepts
- Two's complement negation for 64-bit words
- Isolating least significant set bit with x & -x
- Branchless masking using arithmetic on boolean predicates

## Code Example
```javascript
void isolateLeastBit128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo) {
  uint64_t lo_lsb = lo & -lo;
  uint64_t hi_lsb = hi & -hi;
  uint64_t nonzero_lo = -(lo != 0);
  uint64_t zero_lo = -(lo == 0);
  *out_lo = lo_lsb & nonzero_lo;
  *out_hi = hi_lsb & zero_lo;
}
```

## Example
Input: hi=0x8000, lo=0x0C00 → Output: out_hi=0x0000, out_lo=0x0400
Input: hi=0x0000, lo=0x0000 → Output: out_hi=0x0000, out_lo=0x0000

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to implement conditional data movement without branching. It demonstrates how to construct a full-word mask from a single flag and apply it consistently across 128-bit halves.

## Key Concepts
- **Branchless conditioning via masks:** Using arithmetic to convert a boolean into an all-0s or all-1s mask
- **128-bit representation:** A 128-bit value split into hi/lo 64-bit halves
- **Portable unsigned arithmetic:** Safe, well-defined behavior across platforms

## Code Example
```c
uint128 swapHalvesIf128(uint128 x, unsigned c) {
    unsigned m = -(unsigned)(c != 0);
    uint64_t hi = (x.hi & ~m) | (x.lo & m);
    uint64_t lo = (x.lo & ~m) | (x.hi & m);
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
}
```
